## 1. Concrete techniques targeting register pressure / spilling in an attention backward

First, the arithmetic that frames everything for your kernel, from `bf16_gfx1250_ladder.md:694-697` (stated in four rungs' own spec blocks): **the lane register budget on gfx1250 is `131072 / NUM_THREADS`** — a per-WGP file of 131072 32-bit registers, 32768 per SIMD. Your `num_warps=4` on wave32 is 128 threads, which gives exactly **1024 registers/lane, 1 wave/SIMD** — i.e. your `vgpr_count 1024` is not a compiler choice, it is the whole file, and you are already in the maximum-register regime (ladder rung 11 is the same point: 128 threads, 1 wave/SIMD, 910 VGPR of 1024, 512 of them accumulators, 0 spills). `num_warps=8` = 256 threads = **512/lane**, which halves your budget while your live set is already 1350 dwords — that is the mechanical explanation of the 2.36x, and it means **the warp knob cannot be the escape; only cutting live state can.** Corroborating: the ladder's rung 12 spec block warns that a *spilling* build of a 128-thread-class kernel "hangs after the first launch" (`:673-675`), so your 326 spill dwords are also a stability risk, not only a speed one.

| # | Technique | Source | What the corpus attributes to it |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | **Shrink the accumulator by shrinking the KV band.** FlyDSL's register identity: `dk+dv dwords = D*block_kv/(32*waves)` = 128 dwords at both D=64 and D=128 on their config. Bytes-per-FLOP band identity `1/(5 × BLOCK_KV)`. | `flydsl/attention/recipes/hd128.md` | Their a16 arm at BLOCK_KV 256/BLOCK_Q 32 lands at **512 registers, 10 spill dwords, 20 B scratch**; the deterministic arm at BLOCK_KV 128/BLOCK_Q 64 lands at **462 registers, 0 spill**. Halving the band is the lever that removes the spill. Yours is 326 spill dwords / 1308 B scratch — an order of magnitude past their worst arm. |
| 2 | **Direct global→LDS loads instead of register-staged loads.** Each 16-byte global load in flight costs ≈4 arch registers; an async/direct path *returns* them. | `optimization/techniques/0-register-pressure-and-occupancy.md`; ladder mechanism a2 | One measured conversion **freed ~32 registers**. Ladder rung 02 (`global_load_async_to_lds_b128`) measured **+44.78% [+44.48,+45.07]** here vs HipKittens' published ~1.47x, and it is the **second-largest step on the whole ladder**. Caveat from a2: the async counter **does not retire in order**, so the drain must be full — `wait_async<N>` for non-zero N establishes nothing. |
| 3 | **TDM (`tdm::load_async`) — move the fill onto the engine.** One wave posts one descriptor instead of every lane issuing a transfer. | ladder a3 / `arch/gfx1250/isa.md` | **+40.68% [+40.43,+40.93]** measured on real gfx1250 (vs their published ~1.35x) — the largest single mechanism step on the card, and CDNA5-only. Costs: the stage becomes one deep panel, and **a TDM cannot be EXEC-masked** (tail skip must be a `count=0` descriptor). Not reachable from Triton today, but it is the ceiling your fill path is being measured against. |
| 4 | **Hand-assigned registers (`ducks::art`) + `amdgpu_num_vgpr(30)` + `__launch_bounds__(256,1)`.** | `hipkittens/attention/recipes/gqa_d128.md`, `hipkittens/tiles-and-register-control.md` | **1.72x geomean** at identical MFMA count; compiler-allocated emits **1,638 `v_accvgpr_read/write` vs 128 (12.8x)**, +48% VALU/wave (555,480 vs 375,385), `SQ_WAIT_ANY` **1,243,033 vs 145,049 (8.6x)**, and spills 4 VGPR/20 B. Paper table: **+20%** at seq 4096 (855→1024 TFLOP/s) and 8192 (909→1091). **But `art` exists only under `include/cdna4/` and the AGPR range it exists to reach does not exist on gfx1250 at all** (ladder §13 item 11) — so the *mechanism* is out of scope for you; what transfers is the diagnosis pattern, and on gfx1250 the AGPR half of the motivation is gone. |
| 5 | **Do not chase AGPRs — the whole AGPR section is marked not applicable to gfx1250.** | `0-register-pressure-and-occupancy.md` (explicit), `arch/gfx1250/gfx1250.md:110` | For the record of what it costs on CDNA3/4: attribute-based AGPR request → **1155 accvgpr moves, 43 registers in scratch, 6.2x slower**; forcing accumulators out of AGPRs **~4.5x slower**; AGPR move +5..+13% at 1 wave/SIMD vs −30..−37% at 2. None applies to you. |
| 6 | **The "256 accumulators per lane" cap is FALSE on gfx1250.** | ladder §13 item 12, contradicting `0-register-pressure-and-occupancy.md:107` | Rung 11 allocates **512 accumulator registers/lane, 910 VGPR total, zero spills** out of a flat 1024. The cap is `1024 × lanes`, reached by dropping to one wave, not by stacking two 256-register encoding caps. Do not size your tile against the 256 figure. |
| 7 | **Hoist address arithmetic into SGPRs with `readfirstlane` + `asm volatile("" : "+s"(x))`; prefill per-lane offsets before the loop.** | `hipkittens/tiles-and-register-control.md:156-164` | Reduces the hot-loop load to one `s_mov_b32 m0` + one buffer load. Relevant directly: you have **sgpr_spill_count 65**, which is a separate pathology — scalar spills mean wave-uniform values are living in the vector file or scratch. |
| 8 | **Batch a load class ahead of the loop rather than 1:1 with use.** | `3-pipelining-and-scheduling.md` | **~20% fewer cycles**; 1:1 loads stall **80-95%** of the time. |
| 9 | **Precondition for adding waves:** `current_vgpr × new_waves ≤ 512` (gfx942/950 form). | `0-register-pressure-and-occupancy.md` | Restated for gfx1250 it is `current_vgpr × new_waves ≤ 1024`. At 1024 current, no wave count above 1 is legal — again, the num_warps=8 result is predicted, not surprising. |
| 10 | **Register double-buffering is what usually blows the budget at 1 wave/SIMD.** | `0-register-pressure-and-occupancy.md` | A measured case needed **384 registers against 88 available**. If your Triton `num_stages` is producing a register-resident double buffer rather than an LDS one, that is the first thing to kill. |
| 11 | **`-ffast-math` shifts register pressure in either direction** — re-read the resource report after toggling. | `pitfalls/compiler-and-toolchain.md:134-138` | Unpriced, but free to test. |
| 12 | **Too many early-clobber asm outputs hangs the register allocator** (the shape a register double-buffer produces). | `pitfalls/compiler-and-toolchain.md:99-108` | Symptom is an unbounded compile, not a spill; **no resource report appears at all**. |
| 13 | **"spill == 0" is not sufficient once two kernels share the pool.** | `flydsl/attention/recipes/hd128.md` | FlyDSL chose 4 waves specifically to satisfy `512 − waves_per_simd(body)*alloc(body) ≥ n*alloc(dqred)`. Not your case (you are one fused kernel) but it is why their body stops where it does. |

**Where the corpus says the spill actually hurts:** at 1 wave/SIMD "the latency hiding has to come from inside the wave" (`0-register-pressure-and-occupancy.md:260`), confirmed in the strongest form by ladder rung 11 (a three-slot register ring and hand-placed fences adopted for exactly that reason, §13 item 12). With 1308 B/lane of scratch traffic and no second wave to cover it, every spill reload is exposed latency. Your 549 vs 2236-2667 TFLOP/s gap is consistent with that being the dominant term.

## 2. What the other backends' attention backwards do structurally differently

Three distinct design points; yours (Primus-Turbo fused one-kernel, no atomics) is a **fourth** that none of them ships.

**AITER `fmha_v3_bwd_hd128_bf16` (gfx950, hand-written asm) — three-kernel pipeline, fp32 dQ atomics.**
- Split: `odo` 3.6% / `dqdkdv` 90.4% / `dq_convert` 3.9% / memset 2.1%. **674.1 TFLOP/s.**
- 512 workgroups × 4 waves, **1 wave/SIMD, LDS 163,840 of 163,840, 256+256 registers, scratch 0.**
- dQ via `buffer_atomic_add_f32`, L2-bypassing/uncached: **74.4% of all vL1D requests**, **1096 MiB of traffic for 128 MiB of dQ = 8.5x**.
- **Worth:** switching the dQ accumulation from fp32 to bf16-packed atomics (`a32→a16`) is **24.6% of the main kernel / 25.0% of the whole pipeline**, with every non-atomic counter bit-identical. **`deterministic=True` refuses the asm path entirely: 1.61x slower.**
- **LDS staging:** all 65 vector loads carry the `lds` modifier — Q/dO/LSE **never land in a register**. 512 static `ds_read_b64_tr_b16`, **zero `ds_write` of transposed data, bank conflicts exactly 0**. Even so, LDS Data FIFO full **46.9%** of the kernel and LDS at **60.0% of roof** — "if the atomics were fixed, this is the next wall".
- **Scheduling:** 3 barriers per query step, **zero `s_setprio`**, partial waits `lgkmcnt(2..12)` and `vmcnt(8) lgkmcnt(6)`, only **3 full drains, all outside the loop**.
- **Causal load balancing by pairing K-tile j with n−1−j** (two duplicated loop chains; host does `gdx = (gdx+1)/2`). The card calls this "worth copying" but does not price it. Block-granular causal waste is `1/ceil(S/256)` = **+3.1% at S=8192** — directly applicable to your bottom-right causal shape.
- Wave split along the **key** axis so each wave owns its own dK/dV and the epilogue needs no reduction.
- Overhead: **2.189 non-MFMA instructions per MFMA**, against a GEMM's 0.291.

**FlyDSL / Primus-Turbo `hd128` — your own library, and the closest reference point you have.**
- `dkdv` is 4 waves / 256 threads, 1 wave/SIMD on both arms. Default a16 arm: BLOCK_KV 256, BLOCK_Q 32, q_split 4, kv_halves 2, LDS 116,736 B, **512 registers / 10 spill dwords / 20 B scratch**, 128 `buffer_atomic_pk_add_bf16`, **34 `s_barrier` / 96 `s_setprio`**, 13,954 static instructions. Deterministic arm: BLOCK_KV 128, BLOCK_Q 64, q_split 2, LDS 102,400 B, **462 registers / 0 spill**, 64 `buffer_store_dwordx4`, 35 barriers / 64 setprio, 10,233 instructions.
- dQ atomic image traffic **1088 MiB vs 128 MiB (8.5x)** — the *same* multiplier as AITER, because it is mask geometry: `sum_j (Sq−256j)/Sq = 8.5`.
- **Determinism costs +11.42% at D=64 and +22.68% at D=128 (ratio 1.99)**; the un-permute kernel is 47.6 us (2.8%). Chunked overlapped dispatch hides **87%** of the 861 us reduce — without it determinism would cost ~57%, not 22.7%.
- Upstream docstring prices the whole dQ scheme: dropping the partial store takes the D128 wall **6.78 → 4.91 ms**, dropping the reduce too → **4.18 ms**.
- dK/dV are bitwise-reproducible on both arms because they are split-K over `q_split` cyclic subsets with **one writer per slot**, folded by `slotred` in fixed ascending order — that is how you get determinism without atomics.
- Their **forward** at D=128: 198 VGPR, 0 AGPR, **2 waves/SIMD**, LDS 68,096 B, 288 `ds_read` (192 `_b64_tr_b16` + 96 `_b128`), **zero `ds_write`**. The `waves_per_eu` bump to 4 is gated on a literal `head_dim == 64`.
- ⚠ An `sc0` bit on the dQ atomic is a **silent wrong answer**.

**HipKittens `gqa_d128` — no atomics anywhere, which is the design you are in.**
- Backward: 4 warps, 1 wave/SIMD, 256/256 registers, **0 spill**. Grid is **(H_KV, N/256, B) over KV blocks**; K and V are **register-resident, 64 pinned registers each**; dQ goes to a `[B,H,N,D]` **staging buffer** plus a separate `dq_shuffle` kernel costing **2.3% of the backward**.
- LDS used 140,288 B of a 160,000 B request. **Two swizzles in the backward, because `ds_write_b64` and `ds_read_b128` cannot share one.**
- 146 `asm volatile`, **39 `s_barrier`, zero `s_setprio`**.
- The discriminating datum for you: their **matched compiler-allocated pair shows 8 warps beating 4 by 1.332x geomean** — the *opposite* sign to your num_warps=8 being 2.36x slower. On gfx950 the 4-warp arm spilled and 8 warps rescued it; on gfx1250 8 warps *halves* the lane budget instead. Do not import that finding.
- Three `hipDeviceSynchronize()` cost 2.52% at N=1024 / 0.31% at N=4096. XCD-to-KV-head remap worth 1.74%. Forward's threshold-gated lazy rescale worth 5.3% at a cost of 35.5 dB SQNR (50.68→15.20 at threshold 0).

**Scheduling barriers, cross-backend — and the revoked rule.** The "barriers ≤3 and setprio==0" gate in `0-register-pressure-and-occupancy.md` is **explicitly revoked** by a 7-kernel table; FlyDSL's dkdv backward ships at 1 wave/SIMD with **33 `s_barrier` and 96 `s_setprio`**. Barrier cost is **arrival spread**: measured **11.8 cycles/hit vs 1324 cycles/hit — 112x** between a well- and badly-balanced case. So barrier *count* is not your metric; arrival skew is. From `3-pipelining-and-scheduling.md`: per-consumer partial drains are **+0.7 pts geomean** (neutral to −0.3 when all pools have slack); deleting one duplicate wait was **+2.2%**; **FlyDSL's stagger LOST, HipKittens' ping-pong WON**; hoisting reads on top of the compiler's staged waits was **~20% slower**. Partial-drain safety is **non-monotonic** — `lgkmcnt(8)` failed 3×, `lgkmcnt(6)` failed 13×, and `lgkmcnt(7)` never failed.

**LDS, cross-backend.** From `2-lds-bank-conflicts.md`: fixing 19%→0% conflicts moved time **not at all, twice**; a 0% single-pool layout **LOST** to a 14% two-pool layout (freeing 5,120 B of padding gave ten 16,384 B buffers = 163,840 B exactly, matrix pipe 54.3%); swapping 4 transposing 64-bit reads for 2 128-bit reads on the same addresses measured **80% slower**. You are at LDS 65536 of 327680 — **20%**. Every other backend here runs 85-100% of its LDS limit (ladder rungs 05-12 at 272 KB; AITER at 163,840/163,840; HipKittens at 140,288). **The single largest structural difference between your kernel and all three references is that you are holding in registers what they hold in LDS**, and you have 262 KB of LDS sitting unused while you spill 1308 B/lane to scratch.

## 3. gfx1250-specific warnings

**Profiling (`arch/gfx1250/profiling-surface.md`).**
- **51 counters defined against gfx950's 630; only 13 read non-zero.**
- **The entire WMMA FLOP counter family reads exactly 0**, marked `{emulated}`. You cannot measure achieved FLOP from counters on this part — your 549 TFLOP/s must come from algorithmic FLOP over wall time.
- **There is no byte counter at any level.** No memory-traffic analysis, no roofline denominator.
- ⚠ **`rocprofv3` exits 0 and writes no CSV when given an unknown counter.** Silent failure. Check the CSV exists.
- Stochastic PC sampling and ATT **do** work, interval range 256..1048576 cycles — that is the only rich source on this part, and it is the one that will show you where the scratch traffic is.
- `THROTTLE_STATUS` reads **`N/A` on this part even while pinned at the power cap** (ladder §3a). Do not use it as the power-wall test; use power-at-limit *and* clock-below-`MAX_CLK` together.

**The part is hard power-limited** (ladder §3a, measured under sustained bf16 GEMM): **2497-2502 W against a 2500 W `SOCKET_POWER_LIMIT`, GFX clock 1699-1703 MHz against `MAX_CLK` 2400 MHz** — held **29% below maximum**. Consequences the card draws: **removing work pays and merely overlapping it does not**; rungs 08 (split barrier, 4.3%), 10's `lock_simd` (+1.9%) and 12's interleave are "priced at approximately zero" under a cap, while the arithmetic-intensity rungs (03, 04) and the fetch-*removing* rungs (07 TDM, 09 multicast) are marked up. For you: **reducing spill traffic deletes work and is on the right side of this; clever overlap of the spill traffic is on the wrong side.** Also from `measurement-traps.md`: on a power-limited part a change can save **10% of cycles and 0% of time** with every counter showing a win — and `3-pipelining-and-scheduling.md` adds that under a power wall only *correlated* stall (`SQ_WAIT_ANY`) is real.

**Hardware reference (ladder §3a):** gfx1250 Eng Sample, device `0x75c1`, **256 CUs**, wave 32, 2400 MHz max, **320 KB LDS/CU** (confirming 327,680 B), 432 GB HBM4, 4 MB L2. The 23347 GB/s HBM figure is `amd-smi MAX_BANDWIDTH`, **a nameplate, not a denominator** — the card refuses to quote any percentage-of-roof against it.

**Stability.** **Two GPU hangs in three days on two unrelated workloads.** Hang 1: 25-round campaign hung at round 16/25; `MES failed to respond`, then `failed to send mode2 reset, error code -62` — **the reset itself failed** — then `SMU: No response` indefinitely; needed a **host reboot**. Hang 2: 857 `SMU: No response` occurrences over 11 hours on an op-evolve grouped-GEMM job. Practical rules: **chunk long campaigns** (five `-r 5` runs cost the same GPU time and lose at most a fifth); **budget a reboot**; and critically — ⚠ **a degraded part reads as a slow kernel, not as an error.** The same binaries measured **19% lower** on a long-uptime host (rung 12: 2236.8 vs 2667.5), and *only* on the data-moving rungs 07-12, with nothing in the benchmark output indicating a problem. **If a gfx1250 number moves and the code did not, check `dmesg` for `SMU: No response` before believing it.** Re-establish the baseline in the same session as the thing it grades. (This is also where your two quoted GEMM roofs come from — 2236 is the *degraded* number and 2667 the healthy one.)

**Toolchain.**
- Cluster features need **clang 23**, not ROCm 7.15 as the README claims — they build and run on **ROCm 7.14**. **ROCm 7.2.4 and earlier fail with a misleading `shared_allocator` error cascade.**
- **Neither `vmcnt` nor `lgkmcnt` exists on gfx1250** (ladder §13 item 11). Six wait counters: LOADcnt/STOREcnt/DScnt/KMcnt/ASYNCcnt/TENSORcnt. **clang 23 exposes builtins only for `asynccnt` and `tensorcnt`**; the rest are still inline asm. The entire wait-counter section of `3-pipelining-and-scheduling.md` is explicitly **void on gfx1250**, and its finding that counter-side knobs transfer (+27 units/+0.8%) while issue-timing knobs do not (+21 units / exactly 0.00%) is a gfx950 result.
- **`__launch_bounds__` second argument is minimum waves per SIMD in HIP, not blocks per SM as in CUDA.**
- **Dynamic LDS above 64 KB requires `hipFuncSetAttribute(..., hipFuncAttributeMaxDynamicSharedMemorySize, ...)` or the launch fails at runtime**, not at compile time. You are at 65536 exactly — if you go up, this fires.
- **`sched_barrier` mask semantics are inverted**: the mask lists what *may* cross; `sched_barrier(0)` is the hard fence, larger is weaker. And it does **not** constrain inline assembly at **any** mask, including 0 — inline asm is opaque but is **not** a fence. Order by a def-use edge, not a fence.
- **Matrix instructions inside inline asm are invisible to the hazard recogniser** — and on gfx1250 there is **no interlock at all**: `WMMA_*BF16` requires **5 independent instructions, or 1 `V_NOP` with co-exec disabled**, before a dependent read (`arch/gfx1250/isa.md`). Getting this wrong reads a stale accumulator silently, typically only in the epilogue's last tile.
- **`LDS Size [bytes/block]: 0` is normal** — the resource report counts only static `__shared__`. And a build that prints no spills may simply not have `-Rpass-analysis=kernel-resource-usage` passed.
- **Porting to wave32:** do **not** carry interleave ratios or priority hints across — they are tuned to wave64 and will only disorder a wave32 schedule. The four places code differs: the matrix intrinsic operand form, the shared-memory read shape, the **accumulator store-back row formula** (a wrong formula silently transposes output rows; "the hardest of the four to find"), and the barrier sequence.
- ⚠ **gfx1250 AITER objects cannot be disassembled by that image's LLVM** — empty `.text` for `e_flags 0x549`. If you want to read a gfx1250 reference binary, expect this.
- **"A toolchain note expires, a probe does not."** One recorded "the compiler cannot" blocked four rounds of work before a 60-second compile showed it had been fixed.

**The LDS trap that is specifically aimed at you.** `2-lds-bank-conflicts.md` states plainly: **"gfx1250 is the case where the bank count matching is a trap."** It has the same 64 banks × 4 B as gfx950, so a swizzle looks portable — **but the published phase tables are measured on a 64-lane wave and gfx1250 is wave32, so the lane→phase partition is different and the swizzle must be re-derived.** LDS allocation granularity is 2048 B. Also relevant if you move data into LDS: **one swizzle cannot serve two access widths** (a 64-bit write and a 128-bit read need two buffers — HipKittens' backward carries exactly two for this reason), and the one measured fix of the "row index vanishes" class was widening to 128 elements with `mask = (row & 7) << 4` → 0.00% conflicts, where **widening alone was 5% slower**.

**Two corpus statements that are wrong on gfx1250** (ladder §13, worth knowing since they appear in the files you asked for): the `256 × lanes` output-tile cap (false — it is `1024 × lanes`), and `flydsl/gemm/techniques.md:107`'s `BM×BN ≤ 4 waves × 64 lanes × 256 AGPR = 65536` derivation, which reaches the same 256×256 tile on gfx1250 **by unrelated arithmetic and coincidentally** — the card names this explicitly "so nobody promotes it to a law".

**Measurement hygiene that matters at your effect sizes** (`measurement-traps.md`): order arms **palindromically** (`A B C C B A`) — measured, the same arm read 0.5-1% lower in position 1 than position 9; effects **above 2%** need ~9 paired trials, **0.5-2%** at least 11, **below 0.5%** 11 in two independent sessions with the same sign; measured false positives include an 8-of-9 win worth +0.49% that reversed to 3-of-15. Warm up under **continuous** load for seconds and never sleep between measurements. A JIT baseline needs a throwaway process (150-190% spread otherwise, per `gqa_d128.md`). And: **anything above the architectural peak is a bug report, not a result** — gate every throughput number on the correctness check from the *same* run.

