The merge measured, and it is the round's answer.

**AB: 8.6997 ms, 1.340x `beat`, 1.151x incumbent** — re-validated in place after shipping. A alone 9.5873 (1.216x), B alone 9.4248 (1.238x); superadditive, 13.2% saved where additive would give 9.9%. All arms pass correctness at ≥52.2 dB.

The four failed AB/A runs were not AB's fault. Root cause found and fixed: the vendored tree and the installed `primus_turbo` that `op/baseline` imports both declare `primus_turbo::attention_triton_forward_impl`; two `torch.library` fragments on one `OperatorEntry` means the second one's collection destroys the schema under the first's live `OpOverload`. That is why it wore three faces (internal assert, `std::bad_alloc`, SIGSEGV), why it only ever hit the speed phase, and why it was intermittent. One-line fix: register under `primus_turbo_opevolve::`. My two earlier theories are recorded as wrong in `opt.md` — both looked right for a while.

`beat` held at 11.6543–11.6668 across all four runs, so the ITT scoping did not leak into its compilation.

Route row 4 changed after the corpus read: `r1.i4.g4` ("shorten live ranges") named a symptom with no mechanism and was blocked on an instrument that never worked. It is replaced by **`r1.i7.g7`** — this kernel runs LDS at 65,536 of 327,680 B (20%) while spilling 1308 B/lane to scratch, where AITER runs 100% of LDS with zero spill, HipKittens 88%, FlyDSL 20 B. It holds in registers what all three hold in LDS, and on a hard power-capped part deleting that traffic pays where overlapping it does not.

```yaml
round: 1
route_written: true
added_to_pool: [r1.i1.g1, r1.i2.g2]
top_two: [r1.i2.g2, r1.i1.g1]
why: >
  Both arms are launch-configuration changes, which is the only surface a fast
  round can exhaust, and both were measured alone from op/current before the
  merge was touched. r1.i2.g2 leads because it is the larger single win (5.9%
  vs 4.2%) and because it is the one with a failure mode worth naming: the knob
