## What a fast round is

**It is your judgement, not a procedure.** A deep round runs a template -- five analyses, each
with its charts and its written-up report, whether or not this kernel needed them -- and that is
why it takes hours. A fast round exists so that someone who can look at the operator and decide
what is worth knowing gets to do exactly that. You are that someone. There is no checklist here
you have to satisfy and no measurement you have to justify to anyone.

**The tools are yours.** `rocprofv3` and `rocprof-compute` are both available. Counters, a
trace, a disassembly, a sweep, three builds compared side by side -- take whatever would settle
the question in front of you, and drive it however you like. The corpus's `profiling/` branch is
a reference you may look something up in; it is not a procedure you owe anyone.

The one thing a fast round does not do is produce the deep round's ARTEFACTS for their own sake:
the standard counter sweeps run because the template lists them, the roofline and SoL charts, the
report written so a later round can read it. Those are the template's output, not yours. Measure
what you need, read it, and move on.

**A round that ends with an answer and no candidate is not a wasted round.** If what you learn
is that the thing everyone assumed is false, that is the round's product, and the next one starts
from somewhere better. Write it down and say so plainly.

**Never wait for a deep round to hand you a number.** There is no measurement a deep round can
take that you cannot: same machine, same counters, same tools, same permissions. What it has
that you do not is a template that spends hours producing artefacts. If you need to know
something, go and find it out, this round, and put what it said in `/home/lihuzhan/code/2026_0910__op-evolve/op-evolve/artifacts/gfx1250-attn-llama31-8b-bwd-20260914-015415/rounds/002/1-opt/opt.md` whether
or not it changed your candidate. A question parked for a later round is a question this job
answers weeks late or never -- one instrument on this project sat in the pool through seven
rounds, each of which could have taken it in ten minutes, because each assumed the next deep
round would.

The one thing worth deferring is work you cannot BUILD in a round -- a restructuring of
thousands of lines, or several arms that only mean anything together and take a day to make.
Write that in `/home/lihuzhan/code/2026_0910__op-evolve/op-evolve/artifacts/gfx1250-attn-llama31-8b-bwd-20260914-015415/rounds/002/1-opt/opt.md`, say what it would settle, and carry on. Note the split: if
such a package also needs counters, **the counters are still yours** -- take them now, and let
what they say decide whether the big build is still worth scheduling. Often it will not be.

## Reading the corpus

**You are expected to read it, and step 3 says when.** What follows is the scope -- which
branches, and the one that is off-limits. Do not read it as a reason to skip the corpus: it
is the only source of ideas that does not come from this job, and a run of fast rounds that
never opens it plateaus on its own findings.

`/home/lihuzhan/code/2026_0910__op-evolve/op-evolve/knowledge/INDEX.md` says which file answers which question. Four branches resolve
mechanically from this job and are the ones worth your minutes:

    arch/gfx1250/    ops/attention/    backends/triton/attention/
    optimization/routes/  optimization/techniques/    pitfalls/ before believing a result

**`/home/lihuzhan/code/2026_0910__op-evolve/op-evolve/knowledge/profiling/` is a reference here, not a procedure.** That branch is the
deep round's manual -- rocprof-compute, roofline models, SoL charts, thread traces -- and
each of those procedures costs forty minutes. Do not run them. Do look up a single thing you
need and close it again: what a counter means, which counters name a quantity, the formula
that turns them into a number. Looking up a definition is minutes; running the chapter is the
round.

`optimization/techniques/` is a **catalogue of changes, not a procedure to run**. Take the
change; leave the measurement scaffolding around it. `5-autotune-and-sweeps.md` is the one
exception -- a sweep can be the change itself, and it is fine if it fits the budget.

## Rules that hold throughout

**Never edit `/home/lihuzhan/code/2026_0910__op-evolve/op-evolve/artifacts/gfx1250-attn-llama31-8b-bwd-20260914-015415/job_context/op/current/`.** It is a copy of the best round so far and is
regenerated only when a round is accepted. You work in `/home/lihuzhan/code/2026_0910__op-evolve/op-evolve/artifacts/gfx1250-attn-llama31-8b-bwd-20260914-015415/rounds/002/op`, which starts as a
copy of it.

**Every build and every measurement goes through the runner above.** Not by hand, and not on
whatever GPU happens to be reachable from where you are running. The controller may have GPUs
of its own -- it does here -- and using them silently moves the round onto a different machine
with a different toolchain, which makes its numbers incomparable with the champions the
acceptance rule re-measures. `op/validation.py` is executed through this runner, so a number
obtained any other way is not the number that decides the round.

**A timeout kills the client, not the run.** The runner is `ssh <node> "docker exec <container>
..."` -- three processes, not one. The command inside the container is a child of **dockerd**,
not of sshd, so when a timeout or an interrupt takes the local client the remote process keeps
running, keeps the GPU, and writes its output minutes later, into a directory whose caller has
already recorded a failure. This has cost this project four rounds. One act wrote
`status: not_measured` with the reason "validation.txt is 0 bytes" at 07:10:07; validation.txt
was written, complete, at 07:15:01. Worse than losing the record: the orphan still holds the
device, and the gate that ran next read 116.75 TFLOP/s on an arm the previous round read at
221.57.

So:

- **A timed-out command has an UNKNOWN outcome, not a failed one.** Before writing that a run
  failed, look at its output file and its mtime, and ask the container whether the process is
  alive (`pgrep -af` inside it). "The client was killed at T and the run's own completion is
  unknown" is a true sentence; "the run failed" usually is not.
- **Never launch a second copy while the first may be alive.** Two runs on one device do not
  give one good number and one bad one. They give two void numbers, and the second is the one
  you will believe.
- **Launch long work detached, with a sentinel, so the client's death costs nothing:**
  `setsid bash -c '<cmd> > D/out 2>&1; echo $? > D/rc' </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 &` then poll
  for `D/rc`. Re-attaching is then reading a file. Keep one directory per distinct command.
- **A finished run is not a cache.** If you re-run something after fixing it, make sure you are
  reading the new run and not the old output.

**The framework runs `op/validation.py` itself when you are done**, through this same
runner, against your working copy, and its exit code is the one that decides whether the
job stops. Report yours as you measured it. If the two differ that is recorded rather than
resolved in your favour, so a number adjusted to look better is a discrepancy with your
name on it, and an honest failing run costs you nothing.

**Correctness is the obligation, speed is the measurement.** Use the precision check
`op/validation.py` performs, never one you write for yourself: iterating against your own
tolerance converges on passing your own bar, and the gate will then fail you for reasons you
will misread.

**Measure on an idle device.** `rocm-smi --showpids` and `--showuse` against the *physical*
GPU before and after, both for your own number and for the champion re-measure. Inline, as
part of your work -- there is no preflight step in a fast round.

