#### Take TWO ideas, and measure them apart before you decide what to keep

Not one. Build each alone from `op/current` -- B from the same base as A, never on top of it --
and measure each. Then, **only if neither lost**, build the merge and measure that too. Ship
the best arm you built.

    build A alone      -> measure     correctness, the guard, the scored shapes
    build B alone      -> measure     from the SAME base as A
    merge, IF NEITHER LOST -> measure
    ship the best arm

**The merge is measured, never inferred.** A at +2% and B at +3% is not A+B at +5%. Two
changes that each fit the register budget alone need not fit together, and on a part at its
power cap two changes that each free cycles do not free them additively. If you did not
measure the merge you may not ship it.

**Skip the merge when either arm lost.** A merge cannot be shipped on a component already
known to lose, and the third build is an hour spent on a question already answered. Two arms
is then the whole round.

**"Lost" means below the incumbent by more than the floor spread your own session reports.**
An arm at -0.1% against a 0.4% spread has not lost, it has not been resolved -- and it still
qualifies for the merge. Treating a number inside the noise as a loss skips merges on a coin
toss. When two arms differ by less than the spread, say that the split could not be resolved
at this effect size rather than ranking them; that finding is worth more than a confident
number, because it says this scheme is not paying at the current scale.

**Ship the best arm even when all of them lost.** The round is rejected either way, so the
only thing the choice affects is what the ledger records -- and recording the best of -0.1%
and -3.0%, rather than whichever you built last, is the difference between the next round
reading what this round reached and reading an arbitrary arm.

Report every arm in `arms:`, losers included. One idea is allowed when the two you would take
touch the same lines, or when one falsifies the other's premise -- say which, and why.

#### What mixing two changes costs, so you can decide whether to pay it

Two edits that change the kernel, measured together in one working copy, leave you a number
you cannot attribute to either. That is the cost, and it is what the separate arms above are
for. It is not a rule -- sometimes two changes only mean anything together, and then paying it
is right and you say so in `opt.md`. But when they are independent, the round that measured
them separately is the round whose result the next one can use.

A throwaway arm is not one of the two. An extra build made only to be measured against the
incumbent and then discarded -- a different constant, a padded allocation, one loop unrolled --
never enters the working copy and never faces the acceptance rule, so it cannot confound
anything. Build as many as the question needs.

**And anything that touches no code at all is free.** A static census, a disassembly count, a
