### (d) Compiler knobs named in the corpus that this job has not tried

No Triton env flags exist in this corpus. The LLVM-level knobs it names, with the worth it claims:

1. **`-mllvm -amdgpu-sched-strategy=...`**. `backends/flydsl/attention/techniques.md`: **+0.6–0.7%** on a forward, but **-0.13% to -0.85% on backward bodies already hand-interleaved**. `hd64.md` source comment, measured: *"post-misched is load-bearing on the four-wave fused body: dropping it measures 892.5 against 918.9, and every amdgpu-sched-strategy override is worse still (max-memory-clause 863.0, max-ilp 888.4)."* Expect this to lose on a backward body; it is cheap to falsify.
2. **`-mllvm -enable-post-misched=0/1`**. Load-bearing when on, per the above. Verify Triton is not already disabling it.
3. **`sched_group_barrier` mask-ratio pinning and `sched_barrier(0)`**. Mask-bit table in `optimization/techniques/3-pipelining-and-scheduling.md` and `backends/hipkittens/scheduling-patterns.md` (`sched_barrier_exp_pairs<6,3,1>()`, `sched_barrier_pairs<10,5,1>()`). Note the inverted-mask semantics of `sched_barrier(0)`, and the collision warning: **"cluster" now means two different things in this corpus** — a `sched_barrier(0)`-bounded scheduling region, and a gfx1250 hardware workgroup cluster.
4. **`s_setprio`**. Regime-dependent per FlyDSL; `hd64.md` wraps GEMM2 in `s_setprio 1/0`; the ladder's analogous `lock_simd()` (bit 2 of `WAVE_SCHED_MODE`) is **+1.9% for one scalar register write** — but the ladder card prices it *"at approximately zero"* under the confirmed power cap.
5. **`iglp_opt(2)`**, gated on `NUM_WAVES == 4` in `hd64.md` — which matches your 4-warp configuration.
6. **`-ffast-math`** — named in `pitfalls/compiler-and-toolchain.md` as *shifting* register pressure, in both directions. Not a recommendation, a thing to be aware of if Triton's numerics flags change.

**A hard warning before you reach for any `-mllvm` flag.** `backends/hipkittens/attention/recipes/gqa_d64.md` names four flags that **silently miscompile**: `-fno-offload-uniform-block`, `-mllvm --lsr-drop-solution=1`, `-mllvm -enable-post-misched=0`, `-mllvm -amdgpu-early-inline-all=true`. They produced *the same 159 VGPR / 0 spill report, no warning*, and **SQNR 52 dB → 0.6 dB with ~18% NaN rows**. Two of the four are on the list above. Any `-mllvm` experiment on this job needs a numerics gate, not a register-count gate.

Two more from the same file that bear directly on your spill situation: a `(512,4)` launch-bounds request produced **223 VGPRs spilled, 316 B scratch, 0.187x, SQNR NaN, non-deterministic**; and the rule *"a kernel that writes its own `s_waitcnt vmcnt(n)` has no safe spill margin at all."* Also `pitfalls/compiler-and-toolchain.md`: **`sched_barrier` does not constrain inline asm at any mask value including 0** — inline asm is opaque but is *not* a fence.

**Replacement for the dead counters.** `arch/gfx1250/profiling-surface.md` gives the verbatim stochastic-PC-sampling and ATT commands (interval range 256..1048576) as the only rich sources on this part, plus the discriminating signature you would use to tell which regime your dk/dv pass is in: a WMMA-bound kernel reads **76.5% `ARBITER_NOT_WIN`, 81.9% MATRIX, 82.8% issue rate, mean Wave_Count 1.4**, while a streaming kernel reads **49.7% `WAITCNT` / 41.1% `OTHER_WAIT`, 97.4% `NO_INST`, 2.2% issue**. Given your 128-WMMA-in-1400-instructions mix, I would expect your dk/dv pass to sit near neither, and that measurement is the one that decides between B2/B3 (issue) and B1/B5/B6 (bytes).

